Eight questions, asked of Clarity, answered by reading the repository.

  37 Python files, 3,216 lines

    What happens when the model is down?
      one breaker per provider, in 1 file
    What happens when a tool is wrong?
      a budgeted loop; 9 files carry a step limit
    What does it do when it does not know?
      refusal is a scored behaviour across 16 files
    Where does state live?
      3 files open a database; the rest are pure
    How is it evaluated, and how often?
      120 cases, six layers, on every push
  * Who can see whose data?
      3 files carry a tenant column; 0 enforce it
    What is the cost of one request, and who watches it?
      priced per run and stored on the row
  * How does a changed document reach the index?
      0 incremental paths — it is rebuilt, not updated

6 of 8 answered cleanly. The 2 marked * are real, and both are
structural rather than accidental — they are things the design does not do,
not things it does badly.

And one the eight questions do not ask, which a reviewer should:

  * the eval set is 120 cases from one corpus, 74 of them (62%) document.
    At n=120, a score near 85% carries a 95% interval of 78%-90%: 13 points
    wide, which cannot see a 3-point improvement. §21.6 measured this.
    The fix is more cases, and nothing substitutes for that work.

None of the three is an oversight. Each was a decision to keep the
system small enough to read, and each would be the first thing a team
fixed. Writing them down is the difference between a design and a diagram.
